cuems-utils — notes for whoever maintains this packaging
========================================================

Where debian/ lives
-------------------

On the working branch, beside the source it packages.

It used to live on a separate `debian/bookworm` branch, whose workflow was
"merge main in, add a changelog entry, build". That works only as long as
somebody performs the merge. Between 0.1.0rc14 (2026-08-03) and 0.1.0rc16 the
merge did not happen, so the packaging branch sat seven weeks and 132 commits
behind the source, and two release candidates went out of the source tree with
no changelog entry at all. Both entries were written retrospectively; they are
the ones marked NOT BUILT / NOT YET BUILT.

The old branch is preserved and tagged `packaging-rc14`. It is the exact tree
every .deb up to rc14 was built from, including the build artifacts it tracked
as if they were source. Nothing new lands on it.

This also matches what the sibling repositories already do: cuems-common,
cuems-power-bridge and cuems-nodeconf all keep debian/ on their working branch.
cuems-utils was the outlier.

Building
--------

    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b        # artifacts land in the parent directory

Before shipping any build, check the venv interpreter:

    dpkg-deb --fsys-tarfile <deb> | tar -xO ./usr/lib/cuems/pyvenv.cfg | grep '^home'
    # must be:  home = /usr/bin      (and bin/python -> ../../../bin/python3)

debian/rules pins `dh_virtualenv --python /usr/bin/python3` BY ABSOLUTE PATH on
purpose. `--python python3` resolves through PATH, so a build on a machine with
pyenv active bakes the builder's private interpreter into the package. On every
target host that symlink is broken, /usr/lib/cuems/bin/python does not exist,
and *nothing in the shared venv can start* — every CUEMS Python component at
once. There is no build-time error; it surfaces after `dpkg -i`, and even then
the running process survives until the next restart.

Two further constraints, both of which have bitten:

  * /etc/cuems is shared by at least five packages, including a private SSH key
    belonging to cuems-power-bridge. Nothing here may remove a file this package
    did not place.
  * The shared venv /usr/lib/cuems is populated by several packages. A .deb must
    not bundle anything another package already ships, or `dpkg -i` aborts on a
    file-overwrite conflict.

Planned, not yet done
---------------------

  * Trixie. Packaging currently targets bookworm only. The intention is to
    extend it to trixie and to keep both supported, rather than to migrate. The
    distribution-specific parts are expected to stay small — the venv
    interpreter path and the python3 version bounds in debian/control are the
    two known ones — so the likely shape is one debian/ directory carrying both,
    with the distribution selected at build time, rather than a branch per
    release. A branch per release is what produced the drift described above.

  * CI/CD. Builds are manual today. They should be produced by a pipeline, for
    every Debian flavour the system is expected to run on, so that a package
    exists for each supported distribution from the same commit and none of them
    depends on which machine a developer happened to build from. The pyvenv.cfg
    check above is the first thing that pipeline should assert, because it is
    the failure a human reviewer cannot see in the diff.

  * /etc/cuems first install. This package ships nothing under /etc today, and
    no CUEMS package ships the XML documents a node needs to start. That work —
    shipping the six XSD files, generating the default documents at build, and
    provisioning node identity — is specified separately; see
    specs/planning/etc-cuems-first-install.md and its execution companion in the
    same directory. It will add debian/install, a postinst and a postrm here.

Historical note
---------------

The 2025-era tail of debian/changelog was one entry that had swallowed four
others. Four version headers (0.0.7-31, 0.0.7-3, 0.0.7-2, 0.0.6-0) carried no
maintainer trailer, so dpkg parsed lines 201-232 as a single 0.1.0rc5 stanza and
read those headers as change text. It had grown that way: the file originally
had exactly ONE trailer, at the very bottom, terminating everything above it.

Repaired 2026-09-24 by adding the four missing trailers. THE DATES WERE NOT
INVENTED -- each is the author date of the commit that introduced the entry the
trailer closes, recovered from the debian/bookworm history (908359b, 038295c,
ab6b1ac, 79804fd) and mapping one-to-one. Adding them completes the record
rather than rewriting it, which is the distinction that made the repair
acceptable: dpkg-parsechangelog goes from four warnings to zero, and the
headers and trailers now balance at 14 each.

Builds were never affected -- dpkg-buildpackage reads only the top entry -- so
this had survived a year unnoticed. It was fixed now because the CI/CD work
above will run lintian and parse the whole file, and this would surface as a
pipeline failure at the least convenient moment.

Deliberately NOT changed, and left as known-outstanding:

  * The distribution field on the older entries still reads UNRELEASED, on
    versions that were plainly released. Correcting that would be rewriting
    history rather than completing it.

  * One change block is filed under the wrong year. "[ ion ] Fix circular
    import in daemon.py causing AttributeError" is dated Wed, 14 Jan 2026 and
    sits inside the 0.0.6-0 entry, which is from May 2025. It arrived that way
    in dfc132b, appended to the single terminating trailer the file then had.
    Placing it correctly needs someone to establish which release actually
    carried the daemon.py fix; one change under the wrong year is better than a
    second one under a guessed one.
